.TH OPENNN 3 "2026" "y-- standard library" "y-- Library Reference"
.SH NAME
OpenNN \- Neural network training and inference
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B OpenNN
module exposes three core classes for building and training neural networks:
.B NeuralNetwork
(the model),
.B Dataset
(data handling), and
.B Optimizer
(the training algorithm). It serves as a wrapper around the OpenNN C++ library.
.SH CLASSES
.SS NeuralNetwork
.TP
\fBCONSTRUCTOR\fR
.B NeuralNetwork(inputs, outputs, hidden, [mode = 0])
.br
Creates a multi-layer perceptron (feed-forward network) with one hidden layer.
\fBinputs\fR, \fBoutputs\fR, and \fBhidden\fR define the layer sizes. \fBmode\fR determines
the final layer activation and loss calculation internally: 0 is APPROXIMATION (linear output)
and 1 is CLASSIFICATION (softmax output). The network memory is managed automatically.
.TP
\fBCLASS CONSTANTS\fR
NeuralNetwork.APPROXIMATION (0), NeuralNetwork.CLASSIFICATION (1)
.TP
\fBMETHODS\fR
.RS
.TP
\fBpredict(inputs)\fR -> vector()
Executes a forward pass through the network. \fBinputs\fR can be a vector, list, or tuple
of numbers whose length matches the input size. Returns a vector containing the network's output.
.TP
\fBget_weights()\fR -> vector()
Returns all weights and biases of the network flattened into a single 1D vector. Useful for saving state manually or doing external weight updates.
.TP
\fBset_weights(weights)\fR -> none()
Overwrites all weights and biases of the network from the provided 1D \fBweights\fR vector. The vector size must exactly match the number of parameters in the network.
.TP
\fBsave(filepath)\fR -> none()
Serializes the network architecture and weights to an XML file at \fBfilepath\fR.
.TP
\fBload(filepath)\fR -> none()
Deserializes and loads a network from the given XML \fBfilepath\fR. Note: currently assumes the loaded network is an ApproximationNetwork. Loading classification networks requires verification.
.RE

.SS Dataset
.TP
\fBCONSTRUCTOR\fR
.B Dataset(csv_filepath, has_header)
.br
Loads data from a comma-separated values (CSV) file. \fBhas_header\fR should be true if the first row contains column names.
.TP
\fBMETHODS\fR
.RS
.TP
\fBsplit(train_pct, val_pct, test_pct)\fR -> none()
Randomly partitions the dataset into training, validation, and testing sets using the given percentages (e.g., 0.7, 0.15, 0.15). The percentages must sum to 1.0.
.TP
\fBscale_inputs()\fR -> none()
Normalizes the input features (columns marked as 'Input') to have a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1.
.TP
\fBset_columns(input_count, target_count)\fR -> none()
Configures the roles of the columns in the dataset. The first \fBinput_count\fR columns are set as 'Input', the next \fBtarget_count\fR columns are set as 'Target', and any remaining columns are set to 'Unused'.
.RE

.SS Optimizer
.TP
\fBCONSTRUCTOR\fR
.B Optimizer(network, dataset)
.br
Creates a training algorithm that will optimize the parameters of the given \fBnetwork\fR using the data in \fBdataset\fR.
.TP
\fBCLASS CONSTANTS (Algorithms)\fR
GRADIENT_DESCENT, QUASI_NEWTON, LEVENBERG_MARQUARDT, ADAM
.TP
\fBCLASS CONSTANTS (Loss Functions)\fR
MEAN_SQUARED_ERROR, NORMALIZED_SQUARED_ERROR, CROSS_ENTROPY_ERROR
.TP
\fBMETHODS\fR
.RS
.TP
\fBset_algorithm(algo)\fR -> none()
Sets the optimization algorithm. Pass one of the Optimizer class string constants (e.g., Optimizer.ADAM).
.TP
\fBset_loss(loss_func)\fR -> none()
Sets the error/loss function. Pass one of the Optimizer loss string constants (e.g., Optimizer.CROSS_ENTROPY_ERROR).
.TP
\fBset_epochs(epochs)\fR -> none()
Sets the maximum number of training epochs (iterations over the dataset).
.TP
\fBset_learning_rate(lr)\fR -> none()
Sets the learning rate for algorithms that use it (ADAM and GRADIENT_DESCENT). If called when using an algorithm that does not use a learning rate (like QUASI_NEWTON), it will throw a RuntimeError.
.TP
\fBtrain()\fR -> float()
Executes the training process. Returns the final evaluation error (loss) after training completes.
.TP
\fBtrain_with_history()\fR -> vector()
Executes the training process and tracks the error at the end of each epoch. Returns a vector containing the historical error values, useful for plotting learning curves.
.RE

.SH "SEE ALSO"
Vector(3), Math(3)
